{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>This month shall be to you the beginning of months; it shall be the first month of the months of the year for you.</b> Because the Holy One, blessed be He, showed Moses with His finger the moon in its renewal, saying ״כָּזֶה רְאֵה וְקַדֵּשׁ״ — therefore He said first ״הַחֹדֶשׁ הַזֶּה״, meaning: this renewal shall be to you the beginning of months, so that every time you see the renewal of the moon in this configuration, then it shall be for you the beginning of months throughout all the months of the year. And after He gave a known sign for the days of every month of the year to make them beginnings for all the days of the month that follow them, He said, comprehending the entire month: ״רִאשׁוֹן הוּא לָכֶם לְחָדְשֵׁי הַשָּׁנָה״ — to make the entire month of Nisan the first of all the other months of the year. And He employed ״לָכֶם לָכֶם״ twice, because the first ״לָכֶם״ speaks of the sanctification of the new month, which is not given over except to the court, and it refers to Moses and Aaron whom He had mentioned just before. And the second ״לָכֶם״ is to indicate that the world was created in Tishrei according to the view of Rabbi Eliezer (Rosh HaShanah 10b); therefore He said ״לָכֶם״ to indicate that it is fitting for all the nations to count from Tishrei as the beginning of the year, but for you alone it is fitting to count from Nisan, so that the memory of the Exodus from Egypt should always be between your eyes — like many commandments in the Torah that came as a remembrance of the Exodus from Egypt. And in the same manner that it says (Exodus 20:8) ״זָכוֹר אֶת יוֹם הַשַּׁבָּת לְקַדְּשׁוֹ״ — that it is fitting for us to count all the days, 'one day of the Sabbath, two of the Sabbath' — so that the day of Sabbath should always be for a remembrance between our eyes, testifying to the renewal of the world, for the renewal is a miracle testifying to the One who renews it — so too regarding the months He commanded us to count all the months from Nisan as stated. Therefore it says ״רִאשׁוֹן הוּא לָכֶם״, but not for the other nations, for they have no part in the memory of the Exodus from Egypt. And this accords with the words of Rabbi Eliezer." "And we need to give yet another reason: why did He begin all the commandments with the sanctification of the new month, and why was this commandment juxtaposed to the commandment of the paschal sacrifice? And the explanation for this is that the belief of the Egyptians was that the constellation of Aries (טָלֶה) shepherds all of existence, for ״מַזָּל מַחְכִּים, מַזָּל מַעֲשִׁיר״ (Shabbat 156a). Therefore ״תּוֹעֲבַת מִצְרַיִם כָּל רֹעֵי צֹאן״ (Genesis 46:34), for it is not fitting to say that the leader should be governed by his constellation since he is a shepherd. And therefore HaShem commanded to slaughter the paschal offering, because the constellation of Aries is the firstborn of all the constellations, and through the slaughter of the lamb ״יִפְקוֹד יְהֹוָה עַל צְבָא מָרוֹם בַּמָּרוֹם״ (Isaiah 24:21), and afterward, down to earth it shall come down upon all the firstborn of Egypt. Now the month of Nisan is the time of the sun's entry into the constellation of Aries, and this month was made the head of the months of the year so that the memory of the Exodus from Egypt should always be before our eyes. And one might be concerned that perhaps through this commandment an opening would be given to the heretics to argue and say that it was because of the importance of the constellation of Aries, which prevails in this month, that this month was made the head of the months of the year. Therefore El Ḥai commanded immediately to slaughter the lamb before the eyes of all who see, and to take it on the tenth of the month, in the first third of the month — for then the constellation is at the height of its power, and its 'god' has no ability to save the flock that is under its dominion — and through this they will recognize and know that there is an El who is exalted above all that is exalted. And therefore He prefaced the commandment of sanctification of the new month, because He commanded to take the lamb on the tenth of the month; therefore it was necessary to inform him from which day he should begin counting these ten days. And He said to him ״כָּזֶה רְאֵה וְקַדֵּשׁ״ (Mekhilta), and from that day onward he would count these ten days, to make known the weakness of the firstborn of the constellations, and as it is smitten, all the firstborn of Egypt, who had placed their trust in the firstborn of the constellations, would be smitten. And this is the reason why He delivered to them commandments to busy themselves with — the blood of Pesah and the blood of circumcision — for the blood of Pesah is to nullify the belief of those who believe in the constellation of Aries, and the blood of circumcision because it was said regarding the Pesah: ״כָּל עָרֵל לֹא יֹאכַל בּוֹ״ (Exodus 12:48)." "And Rabbi Matya ben Ḥarash would expound (Mekhilta 5:28): because they had in hand no commandments to busy themselves with, etc. And a difficulty arises against his words — for did not our Sages of blessed memory say (Leviticus Rabbah 32:5): in the merit of four things our forefathers were redeemed from Egypt — in the merit that they did not change their names and their language, and they were restrained from forbidden relations, and there were no informers among them. And furthermore, the difficulty: the expression ״לְהִתְעַסֵּק בָּהֶם״ — 'to busy themselves with them' — he should have said 'and they had in hand no commandments through whose merit they would be redeemed.' But surely even Rabbi Matya holds that they did have these four commandments; however, those commandments are of the type of 'sit and do not do' (שֵׁב וְאַל תַּעֲשֶׂה), and they needed commandments to busy themselves with in active performance (קוּם וַעֲשֵׂה), for our Sages of blessed memory said (Sotah 21a): ״מִצְוָה בְּעִידָנָא דְּעָסִיק בָּהּ מַגְּנָא וּמַצְּלָא״ — a mitzvah at the time one is engaged in it protects and saves. And here they needed salvation as well, so as not to allow the Destroyer to come into their houses; therefore they needed the blood of Pesah and circumcision to busy themselves with, to nullify the belief in the constellation of Aries. And this is what Rashi explained: ״withdraw your hands from idolatry and take for yourselves a flock of a mitzvah.״"], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {1 "The text reads 'אֵין לְאֵל יָדוֹ'; 'El' here is taken in its generic sense ('no god/force has ability'), consistent with the argument about the power of the constellation understood as a deity by the Egyptians. The reference to 'its god' (האל) shortly after appears to personify the constellation's governing power."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:19:47.177773839Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}